Subject: llhttp's JavaScript-host API is compiled only off Linux

llhttp's api.c reads `__wasm__` as "a standalone module whose host is JavaScript" (undici's llhttp.wasm): under it,
the parser's callbacks are imports from that host (wasm_on_*), and llhttp_alloc and llhttp_free make a parser with
them. `__wasm__` names the instruction set. Node's program on the machine is a Linux program, where the compiler
defines `__linux__` for its target, and its llhttp is Node's for Linux, whose callbacks node_http_parser sets
(browser-substrate ADR-0238 §5; Tabnode ADR-0019 §4). The test is made the platform's, the form a fix sent upstream
takes. llhttp.c's `__wasm__` names the architecture (its SIMD over wasm_simd128.h) and is unchanged.

diff --git a/deps/llhttp/src/api.c b/deps/llhttp/src/api.c
--- a/deps/llhttp/src/api.c
+++ b/deps/llhttp/src/api.c
@@ -39,7 +39,7 @@
 }
 
 
-#if defined(__wasm__)
+#if defined(__wasm__) && !defined(__linux__)
 
 extern int wasm_on_message_begin(llhttp_t * p);
 extern int wasm_on_url(llhttp_t* p, const char* at, size_t length);
@@ -78,7 +78,7 @@
   free(parser);
 }
 
-#endif  // defined(__wasm__)
+#endif  // defined(__wasm__) && !defined(__linux__)
 
 /* Some getters required to get stuff from the parser */
 
